as the war in ukraine enters its fourth  month president vladimir zelinsky is  dispelling hopes of negotiations with  russia any time soon in his latest video  address zielinski called russia's  aggression an evil that can only be  contained on the battlefield  ukraine says russia is stepping up its  attacks in the eastern donbass region  with ukrainian troops doing everything  possible to hold their ground  authorities said russia's advantage in  arms and equipment was making the  situation very difficult but in his  nightly address on monday zielinski  remained defiant  russia is very nervous about our  activity  there has been more shelling of  and odessa  they have been more brutal assaults in  the donbas  this is an evil that can only be  defeated on the battlefield  we're defending  this whole region is the most difficult  these are the hardest fights  and let's bring in nick connolly from a  key for now uh nick evil can only be  defeated on the battlefield those are  some fighting words from a president  who's by his own admission uh is having  a trouble getting the arms he needs to  fight the war  that's definitely the case but i think  that was a signal here from kiev also to  the west and particularly to western  european countries that ukraine is  basically not going to be willing to  talk to russia until those russian  troops are forced back to where they  were on february 24th at the beginning  of this war there had been significant  fears here in kiev that there would be  an attempt by maybe france germany italy  when we had that visit of the leaders  three leaders to kiev last week to try  and force ukraine to some kind of  compromises to basically end this war in  a hurry to end this uh spike in energy  prices and basically get back to  business as normal with russia at the  cost of ukraine and its territorial  integrity and i think ukraine feels  vindicated and relieved that that didn't  happen that they were able to make their  case and with support from the u.s and  the uk prevent a kind of diplomatic  initiative from western european  countries into forcing ukraine basically  to  play to russia's terms  but it is definitely a very difficult  situation for ukraine right now they are  able to hold up the front lines and  russia is making gains but very modest  gains but that  disproportion in terms of the resources  russia has some say ten to one in terms  of artillery that is definitely  something that is costing ukrainian  lives  and nick you were mentioning the eu and  of course there was the troika of  european union leaders who promised to  support canada's sea status for the for  ukraine to the european union there's a  decision coming up thursday friday  how would membership when it comes  change the  change the country and what is the  candidacy mean to zielinski and  ukrainians  obviously membership is something that a  vast majority of ukrainian citizens  want all the sociology uh shows that  massive majority for that here it would  basically end decades-long speculation  about where ukraine is going for the  most of its independence ukraine has  basically been a buffer zone between  russia and uh western europe and the eu  and basically has been spent spending  its time rather than kind of developing  its economy uh stuck with these kind of  geopolitical questions so that would put  those to bed once and for all and  obviously you'll be a huge economic  boost and open the door to huge  investment in this country that has  historically underperformed and you know  still has some of the lowest average  incomes in europe and even just being a  candidate would already put ukraine on a  road map forced reforms that would bring  in investments way before eventual  membership we've seen that in poland in  the baltic states in uh neighboring  romania so even before ukraine becomes a  member that would have an impact that  would be felt by ordinary ukrainians  pretty soon  okay nick connolly reporting for us from  kiev thanks for that  since the beginning of the war  volunteers across ukraine have been  doing their part for the war effort some  are turning to recycling to help save  soldiers lives  to find out more about how it's done  dw's rebecca ritters traveled to  southern ukraine to the region around  mika live  this old junkyard is a junkyard no more  when war broke out volunteers took over  the space and turned it into a military  workshop  diemer is a sound engineer and voice  over artist  now he makes flak jackets  bulletproof vests were requested so we  decided to try and make some a small  number thirty pieces we wanted to raise  forty seven thousand herifa for that  but people felt so connected that we  raised 150 000 in the first three to  four days so we made three times as many  so far they've sent more than seven  hundred of the vests to the front lines  places like hardkiv chernahev and  mikolaiv there's recycling involved and  the work looks artisanal but the product  is military-grade  this metal is certified eight  millimeters thick  this rubber from a conveyor belt is  glued on top of it it acts as an  anti-fragmentation layer this rubber  traps all splinters from the bullet  the metal and rubber are bound together  in strong tape and we make holy cross  here  it's a joke that's a joke but i hope it  works  fireproof material usually used to make  sofas forms the outer shell of the vests  just pop the plates inside these  purpose-built pockets and hey presto  well the vest feels pretty good once you  get it on it's quite comfortable  and much lighter than you might expect  much lighter than some of the press  vests i've even had to wear in the field  but the real test of course is going to  be whether or not  it can stop a bullet  the plates deflect shots from an ak-47  standard equipment for the russian army  outside he shows us old tractor wheels  that will go into some of the vests  soldiers arrive to pick up some for the  comrades  they learn what kind of bullets their  new body armor can stop  a quick trip into the shop to take what  they need then they're off to the front  lines with gear to save lives made in  ukraine  russia is tightening its grip on the  occupied regions of ukraine a sign that  it has no intention of relinquishing  control of these areas  the russian defense ministry says it has  repaired nearly 1200 kilometers of  damaged railway in the country's  southeast the tracks link russia with  the occupied areas in the donbass gerson  and crimea it will allow moscow to send  in more troops and weapons  the kremlin also announced that water is  again flowing through the north korean  rather crimean canal the canal once  provided most of the peninsula's fresh  water following russia's annexation of  crimea in 2014 ukraine built a dam in  the town of kalanchak cutting off this  vital supply  russia is also attempting to russify the  population in occupied ukraine to  solidify its rule there it has been  handing out russian passports and  introduced the ruble as the official  currency it's also changed the telephone  country code and is broadcasting russian  television  and i'm joined now by war expert and  former british army officer mike martin  for more on this mike it appears that  russia is seeking to annex the occupied  territories completely what are the  chances of success  good morning  um i i think that their chances of  success are going to be um  bedeviled by the same problem that the  russians have had throughout the war and  that's not having enough troops for what  they seek to do  um if we look around the country at the  moment there are three areas where there  are fighting in the north around kharkiv  in the east in donbass that we hear so  much about and in the south in kharsan  that you were just speaking about in  order to make progress in the east the  russians had to take out troops from the  north and the south and so they simply  just don't have enough troops to do what  they want to do  and there's considerable resistance as  well from the population in some places  behind the front lines how much of that  is going to is a challenge or will be a  challenge for russia  exactly i think it's already a challenge  as well as not being able to hold uh or  make progress on different the different  front lines that they hold uh in the  south around her son  we've seen quite significant partisan  activity  for example a couple of days ago there  was a bomb attack against a  the head of the prison service in hassan  originally a ukrainian collaborator who  switched sides to the russians so he was  attacked and blown up and we're starting  to see  attacks on arms dumps behind russian  lines  they simply just don't have enough  troops to cope with a restive ukrainian  population that doesn't want them there  and if we can just turn to the to the  donbass region for a second ukrainian  military officials admit themselves this  will be a decisive week why is that  well i i would slightly caveat that the  ukrainians have been saying that each  week is decisive in the donbass  and they're doing i think what we'd all  do they're using the battle as a way to  make sure that the west continues with  its arms deliveries  having said that  the pocket of ukrainian forces in the  city of sura donetsk which is what we've  heard so much about in the last couple  of weeks is now reduced down to one  small area so it is likely that the  ukrainians are going to have to either  surrender that pocket or pull out it's  very similar actually to the situation  in mariupol about a month ago but once  they do that  they still hold mr chance which is the  next door uh city which is just across a  river and it's on top of a hill so it's  much more militarily uh defendable  a final question if i may about that  great intangible morale who's winning  the moral war  oh  ukraine without a doubt uh and since the  beginning of the war that's been the  case  that is largely down to one  big factor which is the ukrainians are  fighting for the defense of their  homeland whereas the russians have a  conscript army in some cases they're  press ganging civilians into that army  and the differences in morale are are  completely uh very high for the  ukrainians are very low for the russians  i think you had a piece there that was  an excellent example of that civilians  who were running a factory that made  something else suddenly switched to  making body armor and the ukrainian  troops were coming and getting that body  armor having a coffee and then going  back to the front line these are the  actions of a country and of a military  that have high morale  okay military expert mike martin  thanks so much  thank you  hundreds of thousands of ukrainians fled  to germany after russia's invasion in  february now even with desperate  fighting continuing in their countries  east some ukrainians are deciding to  return home dw news found out why  these buses are taking ukrainian  refugees back home  as the russian aggression shifted to the  east of ukraine more and more people are  daring to go back to safer areas  svetlana haldetsuka and daughters vlada  and zhenya are waiting for a bus to give  i miss home i miss our house i want to  go back  [Music]  we miss friends miss the family people  we haven't seen for over three months  now  and it's hard to be on the road like  that all the time  with some of them it's not even possible  to connect online so i just want to see  them  but their mother svetlana is worrying  about the trip  vlada wants to go home i can't send her  to kiev and stay here myself from a  rational point of view it's definitely  safer here because he cannot know how  will russia act  [Music]  over 800 000 ukrainian refugees have  arrived in germany since russia's attack  in february  but the new arrivals have been  decreasing for weeks  now more people are leaving the eu back  to ukraine than entering from there  my husband is a soldier he's in a  hospital now  and he doesn't have anyone besides us  does that do him  i miss my home my car my garden and i  miss ukraine  i want to see my relatives and ukraine  too but i'll come back because i come  from my goliath and it's close to where  the battle is happening so now i'm just  going there for a short time  some experts now expect circular  migration of the refugees the drivers  bring people in both directions  we drive every day from ukraine and to  ukraine our company sends six to seven  buses and all of them are full with 60  people inside  finally spitlana and vlada's bus arrives  the older daughter zhenya bids them  firewell she will stay in berlin for a  few more weeks and join them in july  provided that is that the war doesn't  make this family and others run from  ukraine again

